<section xml:id="sec-ch03-04-DirectionalDerivatives" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">

  <title>Directional Derivatives and the Gradient</title>

  <note>
    <p>
      As we saw earlier, given a scalar-valued function <m>z=f(x, y)</m>,
      we can compute its rate of change in the direction of the positive
      <m>x</m>-axis (using <m>f_{x}</m> ) and in the direction of the
      positive <m>y</m>-axis (using <m>f_{y}</m> ).
    </p>
  </note>

  <p>
    The next question to answer is, how do we find the rate of change of
    <m>f(x, y)</m> in any direction?
  </p>

  <remark>
    <p>
      When we say direction, we mean a direction in the <m>x y</m>-plane.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch03-04-ex-10-1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose we are standing on the surface
        <m>f(x, y)=\sin\left(y+x\right)\cos\left(y-x\right)</m>, at the
        point  <m>\left(\frac{\pi}{2},\frac{\pi}{4},\frac{1}{2}\right)</m> ,
        and want to find the rate of change of <m>f</m> in the direction of
        the vector
        <m>\vec{u}=\left\langle\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}, \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right\rangle</m>
        (North-East).
      </p>

      <p>
        Geometrically, we do the same thing we did with partial derivatives:
        Intersect <m>f</m> using a plane perpendicular to the
        <m>x y</m>-plane in the direction of <m>\vec{u}</m>, as shown below:
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>

  <figure xml:id="fig-ch03-04-ex-10-1-intersection">
    <caption>The surface <m>f(x, y)=\sin\left(y+x\right)\cos\left(y-x\right)</m> intersected with a plane perpendicular to the <m>x y</m>-plane in the direction of <m>\vec{u}</m>.</caption>

    <sidebyside widths="45% 45%" margins="auto" valign="middle">
      <figure xml:id="fig-ch03-04-ex-10-1-intersection-a">
        <caption>The surface z equals f of x and y, intersected with a plane perpendicular to the xy-plane, containing a vector of the xy-plane.</caption>

