  <p>
    The graphs above show only the plots; you can drag and zoom them. To see the expressions behind them, or to save a copy and create your own version of the graphs, open them in a new page from here:
    <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the left graph</url>,
    <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the right graph</url>
  </p>

  <note>
    <p>
      Since we can now find rates of change in any direction, we have to
      modify our definition of a partial derivative to allow for change in
      both the <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> variables. We call this new derivative
      a directional derivative.
    </p>
  </note>

  <definition>
    <title>Directional Derivative (Geometric)</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Given a function <m>z=f(x, y)</m>, an input ( <m>a, b</m> ), and a
        direction given by a nonzero unit vector
        <m>\vec{u}=\left\langle u_{1}, u_{2}\right\rangle</m>, the rate of
        change of <m>f(x, y)</m> at the input <m>(a, b)</m> (or the point
        <m>(a, b, f(a, b)</m> ) in the direction of <m>\vec{u}</m> is given
        by
        <md>
          D_{\vec{u}} f(a, b)=\lim _{s \rightarrow 0} \frac{f\left(a+s u_{1}, b+s u_{2}\right)-f(a, b)}{s}
        </md>.
      </p>

      <p>
        We call this quantity the directional derivative of <m>f</m> at the
        input ( <m>a, b</m> ) in the direction of <m>\vec{u}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <remark>
    <p>
      When working with directional derivatives, make sure the direction
      vector is a unit vector.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch03-04-ex-10-2">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the rate of change of <m>f=x^2+y</m>, at the point
        <m>(1,1,2)</m>, in the direction of
        <m>\vec{u}=\left\langle\frac{1}{2}, \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right\rangle</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Here <m>(a,b)=(1,1)</m> and
        <m>\vec{u}=\left\langle\frac{1}{2}, \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right\rangle</m>,
        which is a unit vector since
        <m>\sqrt{\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^2+\left(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)^2}=\sqrt{\frac{1}{4}+\frac{3}{4}}=1</m>.
        Using the geometric definition of the directional derivative:
        <md>
          D_{\vec{u}} f(1,1)=\lim _{s \rightarrow 0} \frac{f\left(1+\frac{1}{2}s,\ 1+\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}s\right)-f(1,1)}{s}
        </md>
        Since <m>f(x,y)=x^2+y</m> we have <m>f(1,1)=1^2+1=2</m> and
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>f\left(1+\frac{1}{2}s,\ 1+\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}s\right)=\left(1+\frac{1}{2}s\right)^2+1+\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}s = 1+s+\frac{s^2}{4}+1+\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}s</mrow>
          <mrow>= 2+\left(1+\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)s+\frac{s^2}{4}</mrow>
        </md>
        Hence
        <md>
          D_{\vec{u}} f(1,1)=\lim _{s \rightarrow 0} \frac{2+\left(1+\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)s+\frac{s^2}{4}-2}{s} = \lim _{s \rightarrow 0} \left(1+\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}+\frac{s}{4}\right) = 1+\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}
        </md>
        So the rate of change of <m>f</m> at the point <m>(1,1,2)</m> in the
        direction of <m>\vec{u}</m> is
        <m>1+\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}=\frac{2+\sqrt{3}}{2}</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <definition>
    <title>Directional Derivative (Computational)</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Given a differentiable function <m>z=f(x, y)</m>, an input
        <m>(a, b)</m>, and a direction given by a nonzero unit vector
        <m>\vec{u}=\left\langle u_{1}, u_{2}\right\rangle</m>, the rate of
        change of <m>f(x, y)</m> at the input <m>(a, b)</m> (or the point
        <m>(a, b, f(a, b)</m> ) in the direction of <m>\vec{u}</m> is given
        by
        <md>
          D_{\vec{u}} f(a, b)=\nabla f(a, b) \cdot \vec{u}
        </md>
        where <m>\nabla f=\left\langle f_{x}, f_{y}\right\rangle</m> is
        called the gradient of <m>f</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <example xml:id="example-ch03-04-ex-10-3">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the directional derivative of <m>f(x, y)=4 x-2 x y+y^{3}</m> at
        the input ( <m>-1,-5</m> ) in the direction of the vector
        <m>\langle 1,1\rangle</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The direction vector <m>\langle 1,1\rangle</m> is not a unit vector,
        since its length is <m>\sqrt{1^2+1^2}=\sqrt{2}</m>. Dividing by its
        length gives the unit vector
        <md>
          \vec{u}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\langle 1,1\rangle = \left\langle \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}, \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right\rangle
        </md>
        The partial derivatives of <m>f(x, y)=4 x-2 x y+y^{3}</m> are
        <m>f_x = 4-2y</m> and <m>f_y = -2x+3y^2</m>, so
        <md>
          \nabla f(-1,-5) = \left\langle 4-2(-5),\ -2(-1)+3(-5)^2\right\rangle = \langle 14, 77\rangle
        </md>
        Hence
        <md>
          D_{\vec{u}} f(-1,-5)=\nabla f(-1,-5) \cdot \vec{u} = \langle 14, 77\rangle \cdot \left\langle \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}, \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right\rangle = 14\cdot\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}+77\cdot\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} = \frac{91\sqrt{2}}{2}
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <note>
    <title>Motivation</title>

    <p>
      Suppose we are standing on the point <m>(a, b, f(a, b))</m> on a
      differentiable function <m>f</m>. What direction vector <m>\vec{u}</m>
      will maximize <m>D_{\vec{u}} f(a, b)</m> ?
    </p>
  </note>

  <remark>
    <p>
      Given <m>z=f(x, y)</m>, an input <m>(a, b)</m>, and a (nonzero) unit
      vector <m>\vec{u}</m>; if <m>\nabla f(a, b) \neq 0</m>, we may write
      <md>
        <mrow>\nabla f(a, b) \cdot \vec{u} \amp =|\nabla f(a, b)||\vec{u}| \cos (\theta)</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp =|\nabla f(a, b)| \cos (\theta)</mrow>
      </md>,
      where <m>0 \leq \theta \leq \pi</m> is the angle between
      <m>\nabla f(a, b)</m> and <m>\vec{u}</m>.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch03-04-ex-10-4">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use the work from the above remark to determine what angle
        <m>\theta</m> between <m>\nabla f(a, b)</m> and <m>\vec{u}</m>
        will:
      </p>

      <p>(a) Maximize <m>D_{\vec{u}} f(a, b)</m>.</p>

      <p>(b) Minimize <m>D_{\vec{u}} f(a, b)</m>.</p>

      <p>(c) Make <m>D_{\vec{u}} f(a, b)=0</m>.</p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        From the remark,
        <m>D_{\vec{u}} f(a, b)=|\nabla f(a, b)| \cos (\theta)</m>, where
        <m>|\nabla f(a, b)|</m> is a fixed positive number that does not
        depend on <m>\vec{u}</m>. So the value of the directional derivative
        is controlled entirely by <m>\cos(\theta)</m>, with
        <m>0 \leq \theta \leq \pi</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        (a) <m>D_{\vec{u}} f(a, b)</m> is maximized when <m>\cos(\theta)</m>
        is as large as possible, that is
        <m>\cos(\theta)=1 \Rightarrow \theta = 0</m>. The maximum value is
        <m>|\nabla f(a, b)|</m>, attained when <m>\vec{u}</m> points in the
        same direction as <m>\nabla f(a, b)</m>. The unit vector in that the
        direction is <m>\vec{u} = \frac{\nabla f(a, b)}{|\nabla f(a, b)|}</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        (b) <m>D_{\vec{u}} f(a, b)</m> is minimized when <m>\cos(\theta)</m>
        is as small as possible, that is
        <m>\cos(\theta)=-1 \Rightarrow \theta = \pi</m>. The minimum value is
        <m>-|\nabla f(a, b)|</m>, attained when <m>\vec{u}</m> points in the
        direction opposite of <m>\nabla f(a, b)</m>. The unit vector in that
        the direction is
        <m>\vec{u} = -\frac{\nabla f(a, b)}{|\nabla f(a, b)|}</m>
      </p>

      <p>
        (c) <m>D_{\vec{u}} f(a, b)=0</m> when
        <m>\cos(\theta)=0 \Rightarrow \theta = \frac{\pi}{2}</m>, that is
        when <m>\vec{u}</m> is orthogonal to <m>\nabla f(a, b)</m>. To find
        a unit vector(s) perpendicular to <m>\nabla f(a, b)</m> one needs to
        solve the system of equations <m>f_x(a,b)u_x+f_y(a,b)u_y = 0</m> and
        <m>u_x^2+u_y^2=1</m>. This will result in two vectors
        <m>\vec{u}_2 = \langle u_x,u_y \rangle</m> and
        <m>\vec{u}_1 = \langle -u_x,-u_y \rangle</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <definition>
    <title>Steepest Ascent and Descent</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Given a differentiable function <m>z=f(x, y)</m> and an input
        <m>(a, b)</m> :
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              To maximize <m>D_{\vec{u}} f(a, b)</m> (move in the direction
              of steepest ascent), <m>\vec{u}</m> must be a unit vector in
              the direction of <m>\nabla f(a, b)</m>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              To minimize <m>D_{\vec{u}} f(a, b)</m> (move in the direction
              of steepest descent), <m>\vec{u}</m> must be a unit vector in
              the direction opposite of <m>\nabla f(a, b)</m>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              To make <m>D_{\vec{u}} f(a, b)=0</m> (move in a direction of
              no ascent nor descent), <m>\vec{u}</m> must be a unit vector
              in a direction orthogonal to <m>\nabla f(a, b)</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <remark>
    <p>
      In the definition above, when we say move, we refer to taking an
      infinitesimally small step in a given direction.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch03-04-ex-10-5">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose we are standing on a mountain given by the surface
        <m>z=\sqrt{9-x^{2}-y^{2}}</m>. We are currently standing on the
        point <m>(2,1, z(2,1))=\left(2,1, 2\right)</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        (a) In what direction should we move in order to move in the
        direction of steepest ascent? Give your answer as a unit vector.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch03-04-ex-10-5">
        <caption>The first octant part of the sphere centred at the origin with radius 3 and the point (2,1,2)</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_09_20_467e98fff8bf8066b436g-07. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch03-04-ex-10-5" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_09_20_467e98fff8bf8066b436g-07.png">
              <shortdescription>The first octant part of the sphere centred at the origin with radius 3 and the point (2,1,2)</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The first octant part of the sphere centred at the origin with radius 3 and the point <m>(2,1,2)</m></p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Graph of <m>z=\sqrt{9-x^{2}-y^{2}}</m> and <m>\left(2,1, 2\right)</m>
      </p>

      <p>
        (b) In what direction should we move in order to minimize the rate
        of change of <m>f</m> ?
      </p>

      <p>
        (c) In what direction(s) should we move in order to move in a
        direction of no ascent nor descent?
      </p>

      <p>
        (d) If we take a unit step in the direction found in part (a), what
        point would we end up on on the surface <m>z</m> ?
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The partial derivatives of <m>z=\sqrt{9-x^{2}-y^{2}}</m> are
        <md>
          z_x = \frac{-x}{\sqrt{9-x^{2}-y^{2}}}, \quad z_y = \frac{-y}{\sqrt{9-x^{2}-y^{2}}}
        </md>
        At the input <m>(2,1)</m> we have
        <m>\sqrt{9-2^2-1^2}=\sqrt{4}=2</m>, hence
        <md>
          \nabla z(2,1) = \left\langle -\frac{2}{2}, -\frac{1}{2}\right\rangle = \left\langle -1, -\frac{1}{2}\right\rangle
        </md>
        (a) To move in the direction of steepest ascent we must move in the
        direction of <m>\nabla z(2,1)</m>. Since
        <m>|\nabla z(2,1)| = \sqrt{1+\frac{1}{4}}=\frac{\sqrt{5}}{2}</m>, the
        unit vector is
        <md>
          \vec{u}=\frac{2}{\sqrt{5}}\left\langle -1, -\frac{1}{2}\right\rangle = \left\langle -\frac{2}{\sqrt{5}}, -\frac{1}{\sqrt{5}}\right\rangle = \left\langle -\frac{2\sqrt{5}}{5}, -\frac{\sqrt{5}}{5}\right\rangle
        </md>
        (Standing at <m>(2,1,2)</m>, this direction points from the input
        <m>(2,1)</m> towards the origin of the <m>xy</m>-plane, above which
        the top of the mountain sits.)
      </p>
    </solution>

    <solution>
      <p>
        (b) To minimize the rate of change we must move in the direction
        opposite of <m>\nabla z(2,1)</m>, that is
        <m>\left\langle \frac{2\sqrt{5}}{5}, \frac{\sqrt{5}}{5}\right\rangle</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        (c) We must move orthogonally to <m>\nabla z(2,1)</m>: a unit vector
        <m>\left\langle u_1,u_2\right\rangle</m> satisfies
        <m>\left\langle u_1,u_2\right\rangle \cdot \left\langle -1, -\frac{1}{2}\right\rangle = 0 \Rightarrow u_2 = -2u_1</m>,
        and then <m>u_1^2+4u_1^2=1 \Rightarrow u_1 = \pm\frac{1}{\sqrt{5}}</m>.
        This gives the two directions
        <m>\left\langle \frac{\sqrt{5}}{5}, -\frac{2\sqrt{5}}{5}\right\rangle</m>
        and
        <m>\left\langle -\frac{\sqrt{5}}{5}, \frac{2\sqrt{5}}{5}\right\rangle</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        (d) Taking a unit step from the input <m>(2,1)</m> in the direction
        found in part (a) lands on the input
        <md>
          \left(2-\frac{2}{\sqrt{5}},\ 1-\frac{1}{\sqrt{5}}\right) = \left(2-\frac{2\sqrt{5}}{5},\ 1-\frac{\sqrt{5}}{5}\right)
        </md>
        For the corresponding point on the surface we need the value of
        <m>z</m> at this input. Since
        <md>
          \left(2-\frac{2}{\sqrt{5}}\right)^2+\left(1-\frac{1}{\sqrt{5}}\right)^2 = 4-\frac{8}{\sqrt{5}}+\frac{4}{5}+1-\frac{2}{\sqrt{5}}+\frac{1}{5} = 6-\frac{10}{\sqrt{5}} = 6-2\sqrt{5}
        </md>
        we get
        <m>z = \sqrt{9-\left(6-2\sqrt{5}\right)} = \sqrt{3+2\sqrt{5}}</m>,
        so we end up on the point
        <md>
          \left(2-\frac{2\sqrt{5}}{5},\ 1-\frac{\sqrt{5}}{5},\ \sqrt{3+2\sqrt{5}}\right) \approx (1.11,\ 0.55,\ 2.73)
        </md>
        which is indeed higher than our starting height <m>z=2</m>, as
        expected when moving in the direction of steepest ascent.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <example xml:id="example-ch03-04-ex-10-6">
    <statement>
      <p>Given the surface <m>g(x, y)=\cos (y-5x)</m> :</p>

      <p>
        (a) Compute the directional derivative of <m>g</m> at the input
        <m>(\frac{\pi}{2},2\pi)</m> in the direction of the vector
        <m>\vec{u}=\left\langle\frac{5}{13}, \frac{12}{13}\right\rangle</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        (b) Does the vector <m>\vec{u}</m> give a direction of ascent or
        descent?
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        (a) The vector
        <m>\vec{u}=\left\langle\frac{5}{13}, \frac{12}{13}\right\rangle</m>
        is a unit vector, since
        <m>\sqrt{\frac{25}{169}+\frac{144}{169}}=\sqrt{\frac{169}{169}}=1</m>.
        By the chain rule, the partial derivatives of
        <m>g(x, y)=\cos (y-5x)</m> are
        <md>
          g_x = -\sin(y-5x)\cdot(-5) = 5\sin(y-5x), \quad g_y = -\sin(y-5x)
        </md>
        At the input <m>\left(\frac{\pi}{2},2\pi\right)</m> we have
        <m>y-5x = 2\pi-\frac{5\pi}{2} = -\frac{\pi}{2}</m> and
        <m>\sin\left(-\frac{\pi}{2}\right)=-1</m>. Hence
        <md>
          \nabla g\left(\frac{\pi}{2},2\pi\right) = \left\langle 5(-1), -(-1)\right\rangle = \langle -5, 1\rangle
        </md>
        and
        <md>
          D_{\vec{u}} g\left(\frac{\pi}{2},2\pi\right) = \langle -5, 1\rangle \cdot \left\langle\frac{5}{13}, \frac{12}{13}\right\rangle = -\frac{25}{13}+\frac{12}{13} = -\frac{13}{13} = -1
        </md>
        (b) Since
        <m>D_{\vec{u}} g\left(\frac{\pi}{2},2\pi\right) = -1 \lt 0</m>, the
        function decreases as we move in the direction of <m>\vec{u}</m>,
        so <m>\vec{u}</m> gives a direction of descent.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <remark>
    <title>Directional Derivatives in Higher Dimensions</title>

    <p>
      Everything we have said about directional derivatives in 3-dimensions
      extends to higher dimensions. Moreover, the gradient vector is defined
      similarly.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch03-04-ex-10-7">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the (4-dimensional) function
        <m>f(x, y, z)=x^{3}- y^{3}-z^3</m>. In what direction is the rate of
        change of this surface at the input <m>(1,1,0)</m> maximized?
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Just as in 3 dimensions, the rate of change is maximized when we
        move in the direction of the gradient vector. Since
        <m>f(x, y, z)=x^{3}- y^{3}-z^3</m>,
        <md>
          \nabla f = \left\langle 3x^2, -3y^2, -3z^2\right\rangle \quad \text{and} \quad \nabla f(1,1,0) = \langle 3, -3, 0\rangle
        </md>
        Hence the rate of change at the input <m>(1,1,0)</m> is maximized
        in the direction of <m>\langle 3, -3, 0\rangle</m>. As a unit
        vector, since
        <m>|\langle 3, -3, 0\rangle| = \sqrt{9+9}=3\sqrt{2}</m>,
        <md>
          \vec{u}=\frac{1}{3\sqrt{2}}\langle 3, -3, 0\rangle = \left\langle \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}, -\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}, 0\right\rangle
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

</section>
